  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\n- Dry and warmer through Friday\n\nUpper air analysis shows we are on the back side of a departing\ntrough and upper ridging is nosing into the Great Lakes; surface\nhigh pressure was centered over Lake MI. We should see plenty of\nsunshine today once the last of the stratocu exits the eastern\ncwa; the back edge is near US-127 and moving east.\n\nRidging, both aloft and at the surface will remain overhead for\nmuch of the week. That means dry weather and increasing\ntemperatures. As h8 temps climb from 2c today to 8c Friday, highs\nwill follow suit; we'll be in the lower 50s today and around 60 by\nThursday and Friday. \n\n- Warm and a chance of rain Saturday, then cooler\n\nThe upper ridge will get nudged eastward by the weekend as a\nPacific short wave moves east across the northern US. The surface\nlow generated by this short wave will track from Nebraska to\nnorthwest MN by Friday night. Strong south flow ahead of it will\npull warmer air northward and highs on Saturday will climb into\nthe mid 60s. These temperatures will be around 20 degrees above\nnormal. \n\nThe trailing cold front will move through Saturday night bringing\na round of showers to the cwa. There's a decent shot of cold air\nbehind the front; h8 temps fall from 6c Saturday to -6c Sunday.\nHighs Sunday will be around 50 and in the mid 40s next Monday.",
